20.5  xargs

TL;DR
  - xargs turns its input into arguments for a command, many at a time.
  - Use -0 with find -print0: names with spaces or newlines stay whole.
  - -n 1 runs the command once per item; -P 4 runs four at a time.

      find . -name '*.log' -print0 | xargs -0 gzip
      xargs -n 1 -P 4 curl -sO < urls.txt

Pitfalls
  - Without -0, a file named my report.pdf becomes two arguments: my and
    report.pdf.
  - When the input is empty, GNU xargs still runs the command once. Add -r to
    skip it.
  - -I {} puts the item where you want it (xargs -I {} mv {} {}.bak), but then
    runs the command once per line.
  - To see what would run, put echo in front of the command: xargs -0 echo rm.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 20.5 --why

Related
